                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/11-dimensional+supergravity"} 11d
          %p
            The condition of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+invariance"} gauge invariance
            of an action functional on
            = succeed "-connections" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔰𝔦𝔰𝔬
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{siso}
            = succeed "-connections." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔦𝔰𝔬
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{iso}
            %em unique
            maximally supersymmetric supergravity extension of the ordinary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein-Hilbert+action"} Einstein-Hilbert action
            on a 4-dimensional manifold. This in turn is obtained from the
            %em unique
            (under mild assumptions) maximally supersymmetric supergravity action functional on a (10,1)-dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            by thinking of the 4-dimensional action function as being a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimensional+reduction"} dimensional reduction
            of the 11-dimensional one.
          %p
            This uniqueness (under mild conditions) is one reason for interest in supergravity theories. Another important reason is that supergravity theories tend to remove some of the problems that are encountered when trying to realize
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            as a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalizable"} renormalizable
          %h3#SuperCartanGeometry As a gauge theory – Super Cartan geometry
          %p
            Ordinary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein+gravity"} Einstein gravity
            has a natural formulation in terms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartan+geometry"} Cartan geometry
            for the inclusion of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lorentz+Lie+algebra"} Lorentz Lie algebra
            into the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poincar%C3%A9+Lie+algebra"} Poincaré Lie algebra
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔬
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi d
                    %mo −
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi ℑ𝔰𝔬
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mrow
                        %mi d
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                        %mo ,
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{o}(d-1,1) \hookrightarrow \mathfrak{Iso}(\mathbb{R}^{d-1,1})
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first+order+formulation+of+gravity"} first order formulation of gravity
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} field
            configuration is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartan+connection"} Cartan connection
            with such
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coefficients"} coefficients
          %p
            This perspective directly generalizes to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergeometry"} supergeometry
            and yields the
            %em superspace formulation
            of theories of supegravity –
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Cartan+geometry"} super Cartan geometry
          %p
            After picking a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"} dimension
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d\in \mathbb{N}
            and writing
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℑ𝔰𝔬
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mrow
                      %mi d
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{Iso}(\mathbb{R}^{d-1,1})
            for the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poincar%C3%A9+Lie+algebra"} Poincaré Lie algebra
            %a{:href => "spin+representation#RealIrreducibleSpinRepresentationInLorentzSignature"} real spin representation
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/direct+sum"} direct sum
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℑ𝔰𝔬
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mrow
                      %mi d
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≔
                  %msub
                    %munder
                      %mrow
                        %mi ℑ𝔰𝔬
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msup
                          %mi ℝ
                          %mrow
                            %mi d
                            %mo −
                            %mn 1
                            %mo ,
                            %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⏟
                    %mi even
                  %mo ⊕
                  %msub
                    %munder
                      %mi N
                      %mo ⏟
                    %mi odd
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathfrak{Iso}(\mathbb{R}^{d-1,1|N})
                  \\coloneqq
                  \\underbrace{\mathfrak{Iso}(\mathbb{R}^{d-1,1})}_{even} \oplus \underbrace{N}_{odd}
          %p
            regarded as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+vector+space"} super vector space
            with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            in odd degree becomes a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Lie+algebra"} super Lie algebra
            by letting the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi even
                  %mo ,
                  %mi odd
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [even,odd]
            bracket be given by the defining
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action"} action
            and by letting the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi odd
                  %mo ,
                  %mi odd
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [odd,odd]
            bracket be given by a canonically induced bilinear and
            = succeed "-equivariant" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔬
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{o}
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Poincar%C3%A9+Lie+algebra"} super Poincaré Lie algebra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lorentz+Lie+algebra"} Lorentz Lie algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔬
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mrow
                      %mi d
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{o}(\mathbb{R}^{d-1,1})
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient"} quotient
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mrow
                      %mi d
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi N
                  %mo ≔
                  %mi ℑ𝔰𝔬
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mrow
                      %mi d
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi 𝔬
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mrow
                      %mi d
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathbb{R}^{d-1,1|N}
                  \\coloneqq
                  \\mathfrak{Iso}(\mathbb{R}^{d-1,1|N})/\mathfrak{o}(\mathbb{R}^{d-1,1})
          %p
            is called
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Minkowski+spacetime"} super Minkowski spacetime
            (equipped with its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+translation+Lie+algebra"} super translation Lie algebra
            structure).
          %p
            From this, a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super-Cartan+geometry"} super-Cartan geometry
            is defined in direct analogy to the Cartan formulation of Riemannian geometry
          %table
            %thead
              %tr
                %th
                  (
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+Cartan+geometry"}> higher
                  \-)
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartan+geometry"} Cartan geometry
                %th
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝔤
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
                %th
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
