                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                        %mo ,
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{o}(d-1,1)
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mover
                            %mi ℝ
                            %mo ^
                          %mrow
                            %mn 10
                            %mo ,
                            %mn 1
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                            %mi N
                            %mo =
                            %mn 1
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \widehat{\mathbb{R}}^{10,1\vert N=1}
          %p
            Indeed, all the traditional literature on supergravity (e.g. (
            %a{:href => "#CastellaniDAuriaFre91"}> Castellani-D’Auria-Fré 91
            )) is phrased, more or less explicitly, in terms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartan+connections"} Cartan connections
            for the inclusion of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lorentz+group"} Lorentz group
            into the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Poincar%C3%A9+group"} super Poincaré group
            this way, this being the formalization of what physicists mean when saying that they pass to “local supersymmetry”.
          %p
            One subtlety to take care of is that this makes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super-spacetime"} super-spacetime
            locally modeled on
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Minkowski+spacetime"} super Minkowski spacetime
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            locally modeled on ordinary
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+spacetime"} Minkowski spacetime
            %em superspace constraints
            or as
            %em
              %a{:href => "D%27Auria-Fre+formulation+of+supergravity#Rheonomy"} rheonomy
            \.
          %p
            The other subtlety to take care of is that a key aspect of higher dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
            theories is that their
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} field
            content necessarily includes, in addition to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graviton"} graviton
            and the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravitino"} gravitino
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+n-form"} differential n-form
            fields, notably the 2-form
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/B-field"} B-field
            of 10-dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+II+supergravity"} type II supergravity
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/heterotic+supergravity"} heterotic supergravity
            as well as the 3-form
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C-field"} C-field
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/11-dimensional+supergravity"} 11-dimensional supergravity
          %p
            This means that these higher dimensional supergravity theories are not in fact entirely described by super-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartan+geometry"} Cartan geometry
            – but by super-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+Cartan+geometry"}> higher Cartan geometry
            \.
          %p
            This follows a key insight due to (
            %a{:href => "#DAuriaFreRegge80"}> D’Auria-Fré-Regge 80
            ,
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#DAuriaFre82"} D’Auria-Fré 82
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D%27Auria-Fre+formulation+of+supergravity"} D'Auria-Fre formulation of supergravity
            – that the “tensor multiplet” fields of higher dimensional supergravity theories as above are naturally brought into the previous perspective if only one allows more general
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chevalley-Eilenberg+algebras"} Chevalley-Eilenberg algebras
          %p Namely, we may add to the above CE-algebra
          %ul
            %li
              a single generator
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi c
                      %mn 3
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c_3
              of degree
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 3
                    %mo ,
                    %mi even
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (3,even)
          %p and extend the differential to that by the formula
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi d
                    %mi CE
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mi c
                    %mn 3
                  %mo =
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mn 2
                  %mover
                    %mi ψ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                  %msup
                    %mi Γ
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mi b
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi ψ
                  %mo ∧
                  %msub
                    %mi e
                    %mi a
                  %mo ∧
                  %msub
                    %mi e
                    %mi b
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  d_{CE} \, c_3 = \frac{1}{2}\bar \psi \Gamma^{a b} \wedge \psi \wedge e_a \wedge e_b
                  \\,.
          %p
            This still squares to zero due to the remarkable property of 11d
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Minkowski+spacetime"} super Minkowski spacetime
            by which
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mn 2
                  %mover
                    %mi ψ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                  %msup
                    %mi Γ
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mi b
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi ψ
                  %mo ∧
                  %msub
                    %mi e
                    %mi a
                  %mo ∧
                  %msub
                    %mi e
                    %mi b
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mi CE
                    %mn 4
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℑ𝔰𝔬
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 10
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi N
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{1}{2}\bar \psi \Gamma^{a b} \wedge \psi \wedge e_a \wedge e_b \in CE^4(\mathfrak{Iso}(10,1|N=1))
            is a representative of an exception
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Lie+algebra"} super
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra+cohomology"} Lie algebra cohomology
            class. (The collection of all these exceptional classes constitutes what is known as the
            = succeed ")." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/brane+scan"} brane scan
          %p
            In the textbook (
            %a{:href => "#CastellaniDAuriaFre91"}> Castellani-D’Auria-Fré 91
            ) a beautiful algorithm for constructing and handling higher supergravity theories based on such generalized CE-algebras is presented, but it seems fair to say that the authors struggle a bit with the right mathematical perspective to describe what is really happening here.
          %p
            But from a modern perspective this becomes crystal clear: these generalized CE algebras are CE-algebras not of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebras"} Lie algebras
            but of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strong+homotopy+Lie+algebras"} strong homotopy Lie algebras
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L-infinity+algebras"} L-infinity algebras
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+n-algebras"} Lie (p+1)-algebras
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi p
                  %mo +
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (p+1)
            the degree of the relevant differential form field.
          %p
            Specifically, me may write the above generalized CE-algebra with the extra degree-3 generator
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi c
                    %mn 3
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c_3
            as the CE-algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi CE
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝔪
                  %mn 2
                  %mi 𝔟𝔯𝔞𝔫𝔢
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CE(\mathfrak{m}2\mathfrak{brane})
          %p
            of the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity+Lie+3-algebra"} supergravity Lie 3-algebra
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔪
                    %mn 2
                    %mi 𝔟𝔯𝔞𝔫𝔢
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{m}2\mathfrak{brane}
          %p Now a morphism
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo ⟵
                    %mrow
                  %mi CE
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝔪
                  %mn 2
                  %mi 𝔟𝔯𝔞𝔫𝔢
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Omega^\bullet(U) \stackrel{}{\longleftarrow} CE(\mathfrak{m}2\mathfrak{brane}) \;\colon\; A
          %p
            encodes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graviton"} graviton
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravitino"} gravitino
            fields as above, but in addition it encodes a 3-form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mn 3
                  %mo ≔
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi c
                    %mn 3
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 3
                      %mo ,
                      %mi even
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  C_3 \coloneqq A(c_3) \in \Omega^{(3,even)}(U)
          %p
            whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/curvature"} curvature
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi G
                    %mn 4
                  %mo =
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi d
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mn 3
                  %mo +
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mn 2
                  %mover
                    %mi Ψ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                  %msup
                    %mi Γ
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mi b
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi Ψ
                  %mo ∧
                  %msub
                    %mi E
                    %mi a
                  %mo ∧
                  %msub
                    %mi E
                    %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  G_4 = \mathbf{d}C_3 + \frac{1}{2}\bar \Psi \Gamma^{a b} \wedge \Psi \wedge E_a \wedge E_b
          %p
            satisfies a modified
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bianchi+identity"} Bianchi identity
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/11-dimensional+supergravity"} 11-dimensional supergravity
            (
            %a{:href => "#DAuriaFre82"}> D’Auria-Fré 82
            ).
          %p
            So this collection of differential form data is no longer a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra+valued+differential+form"} Lie algebra valued differential form
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L-infinity+algebra+valued+differential+form"} L-infinity algebra valued differential form
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity+Lie+3-algebra"} supergravity Lie 3-algebra
          %p The quotient
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mover
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mo ^
                    %mrow
                      %mn 10
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi N
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                  %mo ≔
                  %mi 𝔤
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi 𝔥
                  %mo =
                  %mi 𝔪
                  %mn 2
                  %mi 𝔟𝔯𝔞𝔫𝔢
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi 𝔬
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mrow
                      %mn 10
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi N
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\widehat{\mathbb{R}}^{10,1|N=1}
                  \\coloneqq
                  \\mathfrak{g}/\mathfrak{h}
                  \=
                  \\mathfrak{m}2\mathfrak{brane} / \mathfrak{o}(\mathbb{R}^{10,1|N=1})
          %p
            is known as
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extended+super+Minkowski+spacetime"} extended super Minkowski spacetime
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+integration"} Lie integration
            of this is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+infinity-group"} smooth 3-group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            which receives a map from the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lorentz+group"} Lorentz group
          %p
            This means that a global description of the geometry which (
            %a{:href => "#CastellaniDAuriaFre91"}> Castellani-D’Auria-Fré 91
            ) discuss locally on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/charts"} charts
            has to be a higher kind of Cartan geometry which is locally modeled not just on
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosets"} cosets
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+quotients"} homotopy quotients
            of (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+infinity-group"}> smooth
            ,
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+super+infinity-groupoid"} supergeometric
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinity-groups"} infinity-groups
            –
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+Cartan+geometry"} higher Cartan geometry
          %h3#SolutionsWithGlobalSupersymmetry Solutions with global supersymmetry
          %p
            A solution to the bosonic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein+equations"} Einstein equations
            of ordinary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            – some
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+manifold"} Riemannian manifold
            – has a
            %em global symmetry
            if it has a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Killing+vector"} Killing vector
          %p
            Accordingly, a configuration that solves the supergravity
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euler-Lagrange+equations"} Euler-Lagrange equations
            is a
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/global+supersymmetry"} global supersymmetry
            if it has a
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Killing+spinor"} Killing spinor
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covariantly+constant+spinor"} covariantly constant spinor
          %p
            Here the notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covariant+derivative"} covariant derivative
            includes the usual
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Levi-Civita+connection"} Levi-Civita connection
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/torsion"} torsion
            components and contributions from other
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/background+gauge+fields"} background gauge fields
            such as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kalb-Ramond+field"} Kalb-Ramond field
            and the
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/RR-field"} RR-field
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+II+supergravity"} type II supergravity
            or
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/heterotic+string+theory"} heterotic supergravity
          %p
            Of particular interest to phenomenologists around the turn of the millennium (but maybe less so today with new experimental evidence) has been in solutions of spacetime manifolds of the form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi M
                    %mn 4
                  %mo ×
                  %msup
                    %mi Y
                    %mn 6
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M^4 \times Y^6
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi M
                    %mn 4
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M^4
            the locally observed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+spacetime"} Minkowski spacetime
            (that plays a role as the background for all available particle accelerator experiments) and a small closed 6-dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+manifold"} Riemannian manifold
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Y
                      %mn 6
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y^6
          %p
            In the absence of further fields besides gravity, the condition that such a configuration has precisely one
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Killing+spinor"} Killing spinor
            and hence precisely one
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/global+supersymmetry"} global supersymmetry
            turns out to be precisely that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Y
                    %mn 6
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y^6
            is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Calabi-Yau+manifold"} Calabi-Yau manifold
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            comes from. (Notice though that nothing in the theory itself demands such a compactification. It is only the phenomenological assumption of the factorized spacetime compactification together with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N = 1
            supersymmetry that does so.)
          %p
            More generally, in the presence of other
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/background+gauge+field"} background gauge field
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+Calabi-Yau+space"} generalized Calabi-Yau space
            = succeed "))." do
              %a{:href => "#GMPT"} GMPT05
          %p For more see
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supersymmetry+and+Calabi-Yau+manifolds"} supersymmetry and Calabi-Yau manifolds
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#as_a_background_for_greenschwarz_models
            As a background for Green-Schwarz
            = succeed "-models" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equations+of+motion"} equations of motion
            of those theories of supergravity which qualify as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/target+spaces"} target spaces
            for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Green-Schwarz+action+functional"} Green-Schwarz action functional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma+models"} sigma models
            (e.g. 10d
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/heterotic+supergravity"} heterotic supergravity
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/heterotic+string"} heterotic string
            and 10d
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+II+supergravity"} type II supergravity
            for the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+II+string"} type II string
            = succeed "-models" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/WZW-model"} WZW-model
            term being well defined, hence
            = succeed "-symmetry" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi κ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \kappa
            %em
              %a{:href => "Green-Schwarz+action+functional#ReferencesSupergravityBackgroundEquationsOfMotion"} Green-Schwarz action – References – Supergravity equations of motion
            for pointers.
          %h3#UDuality
            Scalar moduli spaces and
            = succeed "-duality" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+space"} compact
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exceptional+Lie+groups"} exceptional Lie groups
            form a series
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi E
                    %mn 8
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi E
                    %mn 7
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi E
                    %mn 6
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  E_8, E_7, E_6
          %p which is usefully thought of to continue as
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi E
                    %mn 5
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %mi Spin
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 10
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi E
                    %mn 4
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %mi SU
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 5
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi E
                    %mn 3
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %mi SU
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 3
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ×
                  %mi SU
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  E_5 := Spin(10), E_4 := SU(5), E_3 := SU(3) \times SU(2)
                  \\,.
          %p
            Supergravity theories are controled by the corresponding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/split+real+forms"} split real forms
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi E
                    %mrow
                      %mn 8
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 8
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi E
                    %mrow
                      %mn 7
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 7
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi E
                    %mrow
                      %mn 6
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 6
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  E_{8(8)}, E_{7(7)}, E_{6(6)}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi E
                    %mrow
                      %mn 5
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 5
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %mi Spin
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 5
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 5
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi E
                    %mrow
                      %mn 4
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 4
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %mi SL
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 5
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi E
                    %mrow
                      %mn 3
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 3
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %mi SL
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 3
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ×
                  %mi SL
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 2
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  E_{5(5)} := Spin(5,5), E_{4(4)} := SL(5, \mathbb{R}),
                  E_{3(3)} := SL(3, \mathbb{R}) \times SL(2, \mathbb{R})
                  \\,.
          %p
            For instance the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scalar+fields"} scalar fields
            in the field
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supermultiplet"} supermultiplet
            of
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 3
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi d
                    %mo ≤
                    %mn 11
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 3 \leq d \leq 11
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+spaces"} moduli spaces
            parameterized by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homogeneous+spaces"} homogeneous spaces
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi E
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %msub
                    %mi K
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  E_{n(n)}/ K_n
          %p for
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo =
                  %mn 11
                  %mo −
                  %mi d
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  n = 11 - d
                  \\,,
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi K
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K_n
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/maximal+compact+subgroup"} maximal compact subgroup
            of
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi E
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E_{n(n)}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi K
                    %mn 8
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi Spin
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 16
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi K
                    %mn 7
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi SU
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 8
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi K
                    %mn 6
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi Sp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 4
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  K_8 \simeq Spin(16), K_7 \simeq SU(8), K_6 \simeq Sp(4)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi K
                    %mn 5
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi Spin
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 5
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ×
                  %mi Spin
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 5
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi K
                    %mn 4
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi Spin
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 5
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi K
                    %mn 3
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi SU
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ×
                  %mi SO
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  K_5 \simeq Spin(5) \times Spin(5),
                  K_4 \simeq Spin(5),
                  K_3 \simeq SU(2) \times SO(2)
                  \\,.
          %p
            Therefore
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi E
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E_{n(n)}
            acts as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/global+symmetry"} global symmetry
            on the supergravity fields.
          %p
            This is no longer quite true for their
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/UV-completion"} UV-completion
            by the corresponding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kaluza-Klein+mechanism"} compactifications
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            (e.g.
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+II+string+theory"} type II string theory
            for
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+II+supergravity"} type II supergravity
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+group"} discrete subgroup
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi E
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ↪
                  %msub
                    %mi E
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  E_{n(n)}(\mathbb{Z}) \hookrightarrow E_{n(n)}
          %p
            acts as global symmetry. This is called the
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/U-duality"} U-duality
            group of the supergravity theory (see there for more).
          %p
            It has been argued that this pattern should continue in some way further to the remaining values
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi d
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mn 3
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \leq d \lt 3
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kac-Moody+groups"} Kac-Moody groups
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kac-Moody+algebras"} Kac-Moody algebras
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔢
                    %mn 9
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔢
                    %mn 10
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔢
                    %mn 11
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathfrak{e}_9, \mathfrak{e}_10, \mathfrak{e}_{11}
                  \\,.
          %p
            Continuing in the other direction to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mo =
                  %mn 10
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d = 10
            (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n = 1
            ) connects to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/T-duality"} T-duality
            group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi O
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi d
                  %mo ,
                  %mi d
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} O(d,d,\mathbb{Z})
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+II+string+theory"} type II string theory
          %p
            See the references (
            %a{:href => "#UDualityReferences"}> below
